WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.201
Idea. Your friend's cake stall sells out every weekend. So she opens a second stall across town. That is a firm growing: more outlets, more customers, more sales.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:25.016
Firms can also grow by joining with others. A merger is when two firms agree to join into one. A takeover is when one firm buys control of another, usually by buying over half its shares.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:35.087
Growing needs finance: the money a firm uses to run and expand. It can come from retained profit, bank loans or the owner's savings.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:47.488
Method. Step one, know the factors that influence growth: access to finance, economies of scale, spreading risk, taking over competitors, and government regulation.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:59.779
Step two, develop how the factor drives growth. Finance pays for expansion. Scale lowers unit cost. More places spread risk. Buying a rival raises market share.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:10.972
Step three, for a twelve-mark Evaluate, weigh benefits against risks: borrowing costs, diseconomies of scale, and weak demand in the new place. Then conclude.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:29.411
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2024. Anastasia runs a martial arts club in District sixteen of Budapest. After a successful few years, she may open a second club. Analyse two possible factors. Six marks.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.100
First, the case facts. One club, six classes a week, children and adults, and a successful few years.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:50.101
Factor one, access to finance. The club has done well, so it probably has profit to reinvest, and banks are more willing to lend. That money pays for a building, mats and instructors.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:02:02.739
Factor two, spreading risk. All her income comes from District sixteen. If a rival opened nearby, revenue could fall sharply. A club in another district spreads that risk.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:12.258
Both factors make a second club attractive for Anastasia. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.159
Common mistake. A common mistake is mixing up a merger and a takeover.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.370
Another is naming a factor without saying how it affects the decision to grow.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:37.657
Your turn. Now you try. Priya owns a busy phone repair shop. A rival repair shop on the same street is for sale. Explain one reason why Priya might buy it. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:51.021
Answer. Taking over a competitor. If Priya buys the rival, there is one less shop competing for customers on her street. Her market share rises, and she gains the rival's profits too.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:59.735
Remember: pick the factor the case supports, show how it drives growth, and use the case facts. Now try the practice questions.
